RANSAC -- bincv::estimateAffine2D against cv::estimateAffine2D(RANSAC)
======================================================================
OpenCV 4.10.0, cv::getNumThreads() = 1; binCV is single-threaded
threshold 3.0 px, confidence 0.99, iteration cap 2000, seeded and deterministic

 HEAP PROBE SELF-CHECK -- known answers it must recover
   sees malloc, and free returns the bytes        ok -- peak 100008 B, released 100008 B
   sees operator new                              ok -- peak 50008 B
   peak live is not the sum of allocations        ok -- peak 10008 B over 10 calls
   balanced work nets to zero                     ok -- net 0 B

================ 200 correspondences, 30% outliers ================
 planted inliers 140 | binCV 140 (11 iterations) | OpenCV 140

 PEAK SIMULTANEOUSLY-LIVE HEAP OF ONE CALL (allocator-level; sees cv::fastMalloc)
   binCV          0 B   plus 56 B of caller-owned scratch, which is known
                     before the call via ransacScratchBytes()
   OpenCV     27256 B   allocated internally; not visible from its signature
   -> 486.7x smaller, counting binCV's scratch against OpenCV's peak live

 ALLOCATOR TRAFFIC DURING ONE CALL -- A SEPARATE CLAIM FROM THE ABOVE
   binCV       0 calls   the caller's buffer is reused across frames
   OpenCV     60 calls   per call, so ~1200/second in a 20 Hz frame loop
   Peak live says how many bytes must exist at once; traffic says how
   often the allocator is entered. Neither substitutes for the other.

 variant                       us/call   spread   vs OpenCV
 binCV                           18.53     0.1%      11.49x
 cv::estimateAffine2D           212.83     0.2%       1.00x
 NOTE: both sides refit over their consensus set, so this compares the
 same work. binCV's refit is a closed-form least squares; OpenCV's is a
 bounded Levenberg-Marquardt, which for a model linear in its parameters
 converges to the same point. Accuracy against a planted transform is
 asserted in tests/test_ransac.cpp, not here.

